PR #869  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: YSEAL silently clears ctag when cs2 carries properties incompatible with the requested seal_type, even though the type is architecturally valid — software assuming absolute seal_type validity loses compartmentalization guarantees
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/switch_to.h:84-89  __switch_to_envcfg
      ALTERNATIVE-patched unconditional csrw CSR_ENVCFG on every context switch with zero read-back. Currently safe because ENVCFG bits (CBZE, CBCFE, SSE, LPE, PMM) are validated at boot-time WARL probing, but the pattern — csrw without read-back in the hottest kernel path — is exactly what would silently discard a Zysseal seal outcome. A Zysseal CSR write inserted here would never detect ctag=0.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/switch_to.h:73-82  envcfg_update_bits
      Writes thread.envcfg in memory then csr_write(CSR_ENVCFG) with zero read-back when task==current. The 'software believes state committed, hardware may have silently cleared bits' pattern. Currently safe because envcfg bits are pre-validated at boot, but the function signature (accepting arbitrary mask/val) has no guard against WARL-rejected bits being passed by future callers — exactly the Zysseal hazard.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu.c:578-623  kvm_arch_vcpu_load
      Writes 15+ CSRs including CSR_HENVCFG (line 615) and CSR_HSTATEEN0 with no read-back. The henvcfg.ADUE bit is documented at vcpu_onereg.c:164 as 'read-only zero if menvcfg.ADUE is zero', yet this code writes cfg->henvcfg unconditionally. If menvcfg.ADUE=0 at load time (e.g., changed by firmware or another VM), the ADUE=1 write is silently rejected. Software believes the guest has ADUE enabled; hardware may have it disabled. This is not hypothetical — the companion comment at vcpu_onereg.c:163-166 proves developers know about this exact WARL correlation hazard.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu.c:543-576  kvm_riscv_vcpu_setup_config
      Builds cfg->henvcfg and cfg->hstateen0 entirely from guest ISA bitmap via riscv_isa_extension_available() — validates against an abstract ISA table then commits to in-memory config without any CSR-level encodability probe. The SVADU branch (lines 560-562) sets ENVCFG_ADUE based on guest ISA having SVADU and lacking SVADE, but never checks whether menvcfg.ADUE is actually set on this physical hart. The config is consumed by kvm_arch_vcpu_load which writes it without read-back. This is the exact 'validate seal_type against global legal-types table' pattern from the Zysseal spec.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/usercfi.c:62-75  set_shstk_status
      Validates via software-level capability check (is_user_shstk_enabled()), manipulates envcfg SSE bit in memory, then csr_write(CSR_ENVCFG) with no read-back. The SSE bit may be WARL and silently rejected on a hart where the extension is present but the particular encoding is unsupported. The sibling function set_shstk_lock() (lines 77-80) is a 'seal' semantic — sets ubcfi_locked=1, irreversible — with zero hardware back-verification, which is the direct analogue of YSEAL seal-then-assume-success.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/usercfi.c:92-105  set_indir_lp_status
      Identical silent-failure pattern to set_shstk_status: validates feature availability via is_user_lpad_enabled(), sets ENVCFG_LPE bit in memory, writes CSR with no read-back. Two instances of the same bug class in the same file, plus set_indir_lp_lock() with the same irreversible-seal-without-verification pattern, form a systematic pattern that would be replicated for a Zysseal handler.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/suspend.c:44-63  suspend_restore_csrs
      Restores envcfg via csr_write(CSR_ENVCFG, context->envcfg) after suspend with no read-back. If firmware modified menvcfg during suspend (e.g., SBI firmware update, secure monitor reconfiguration), the restore silently writes bits that are now RO-zero, creating a 'seal restored but capability encoding silently broken' scenario. The saved context->envcfg encodes the pre-suspend state; post-resume menvcfg may differ. No csr_read(CSR_ENVCFG) follows the write to confirm the bits stuck. This is particularly dangerous because suspend/resume is rare and firmware state changes are invisible to kernel validation at suspend time.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/cpufeature.c:1171-1186  riscv_user_isa_enable
      Validates Zicboz/Zicbom via riscv_has_extension_unlikely() (a lookup in the global resolved_isa bitmap, not a CSR probe), then sets envcfg bits in current->thread.envcfg. The CSR write is deferred to context switch (via __switch_to_envcfg or envcfg_update_bits), neither of which read back. A Zysseal entry following this exact pattern would validate against the bitmap, set a thread.envcfg bit, and assume the deferred csrw succeeds — with no hardware confirmation.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/cpufeature.c:625-666  riscv_resolve_isa
      The core validate-against-table-then-commit loop. Each ISA extension's validate callback (ext->validate) can check DT properties, SBI extensions, or dependencies — but never probes CSR-level encodability. After validation, set_bit(bit, resolved_isa) commits the extension with no hardware CSR probe. A Zysseal entry added here would follow this path: the validate callback could check anything except the CSR's WARL behavior, and the bit would be set unconditionally, producing a false-sealing-success state.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/cpufeature.c:492-593  riscv_isa_ext
      The global 'legal types' table. Adding a Zysseal entry here with __RISCV_ISA_EXT_DATA or __RISCV_ISA_EXT_DATA_VALIDATE would encode the 'seal_type is legal in the abstract' assumption — the fundamental violation condition from the security spec. The table structure (bitmap-based, with optional software-level validate callbacks) cannot represent per-capability encodability constraints. This is the structural root of the pattern: the data model asserts extension presence/absence as a global Boolean, but Zysseal sealing eligibility is encoding-dependent and not absolute.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_onereg.c:154-175  kvm_riscv_vcpu_isa_enable_allowed
      The SVADU case (lines 162-167) explicitly documents that 'henvcfg.ADUE is read-only zero if menvcfg.ADUE is zero' — proving developers are aware of WARL CSR bit correlations — yet only checks via arch_has_hw_pte_young() (a software-level feature flag, not a CSR read). The comment is a documented admission of the hazard: it describes the exact mechanism (menvcfg→henvcfg WARL correlation) but the code doesn't probe the hardware, relying instead on a software-level proxy. This is the documented-but-unfixed pattern that Zysseal would inherit: validate against abstract condition, never probe CSR-level encodability.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/process.c:399-403  try_to_set_pmm
      The ONE location that properly verifies WARL CSR writes: csr_set followed by csr_read_clear to confirm which bits actually stuck. Returns true only if the written value matches the read-back. This proves developers understand the WARL verification hazard and know the fix (read-back after write) but apply it selectively — only for PMM. A Zysseal handler could easily be authored without this pattern, matching the spec's bug scenario. This is the positive counter-example demonstrating the fix exists but is not systematically applied.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/process.c:423-441  tagged_addr_init
      Calls try_to_set_pmm for WARL probing but only for the PMM field. The comment 'Assume the supported PMLEN values are the same on all harts' (line 430) is the cross-hart assumption hazard: any per-capability encodability variance (like YSEAL's cs2-dependent encoding) would be missed by a single-boot-hart probe. The WARL detection pattern exists (try_to_set_pmm) but is scoped to one field on one hart at boot time — false-confidence scenario for anything with hart-specific or capability-specific encodability constraints.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:567-573  csr_write
      The csrw macro discards the CSR write result — the root enabler of all silent-failure patterns. The inline asm volatile performs csrw with no output operand, so the prior CSR value (and any indication of WARL rejection) is lost. Contrast with csr_read_set (line 575, uses csrrs with =r output) and csr_read_clear (line 592, uses csrrc with =r output), which DO capture the old CSR value and thus enable read-back verification. A Zysseal CSR write using this macro would silently discard the sealing outcome — ctag=0 indistinguishable from ctag=1 at the software level.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/hwcap.h:106-119  
      Extension ID bitmap space. A gap exists between ZICFISS=105 and XLINUXENVCFG=127 where Zysseal could be allocated. The purely Boolean bitmap (present/absent per bit) cannot represent encoding-internal correlations between seal_type encodability and other capability fields (permissions, bounds, reserved bits). A Zysseal bit here could only encode 'extension present' — not 'sealing eligible on this specific cs2'. This structural limitation constrains how Zysseal could be represented at the kernel's ISA data-model level, and any kernel code consuming this bitmap (all of cpufeature.c, kvm, usercfi.c) would inherit the assumption that extension presence implies operational success.

