  [RISK] sys/kern/subr_capability.c:289-315  cap_rights_is_valid
      Validates rights masks purely against abstract format constraints (version, array size, CAP_ALL containment, index self-consistency). On non-CHERI targets this is sufficient because the uint64_t array is the canonical representation with no further encoding step. However, under CHERI, a rights mask passing all these abstract checks could be unencodable in a hardware capability register due to encoding-internal correlations between permission bits and reserved/address-space fields. The invariant 'target architecture has no capability encoding constraints' is true now (__CHERI__ is undefined, __intcap_t collapses to __intptr_t) but is not enforced by any compile-time or run-time assertion in this function.
  [RISK] sys/kern/subr_capability.c:317-338  cap_rights_merge
      Bitwise-ORs two individually-valid rights masks (asserted at lines 325-326) and asserts the result is abstract-valid (line 335). However, cap_rights_is_valid at line 335 only checks abstract format correctness. Under CHERI, the union of two individually-encodable permission sets need not be co-encodable in a single hardware capability — permission bit positions may conflict with seal_type or reserved fields. The post-merge assertion (line 335) would pass for a rights set that cap_rights_is_valid deems legal but that CHERI hardware would silently degrade to ctag=0. Safe only because the current target is non-CHERI (implicit invariant, not code-enforced).
  [RISK] sys/kern/sys_capability.c:229-261  kern_cap_rights_limit
      This is the materialization point: after _cap_check (line 245, which only calls cap_rights_contains for abstract containment), the new rights mask is written directly to fdep->fde_rights (line 248) under seqc_write_begin/end. Under CHERI, this assignment would be the point where an abstract-valid but hardware-unencodable rights set is stored into a filedescent structure that may later be materialized into a CHERI capability. The cleared-ctag failure would occur silently at the point of hardware capability construction, not here. No CHERI-encodability guard exists before the write. Safe only because the current target lacks CHERI hardware capability types.
  [RISK] sys/kern/sys_capability.c:193-207  cap_rights_to_vmprot
